WEBVTT

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:01.920
Robocop 2!

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:04.520
He's back to protect the innocent!

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:27.760
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:29.560
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:31.040
And I'm Joe.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:33.640
Wow, the whole gang together!

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:38.120
The whole gang! Joe, we have something to tell you.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:44.000
Don't tell me that Robocop is secretly a Nobunaga's ambition type game.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:45.700
Well, no, no, no.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:47.120
It's a Nobunaga-like.

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:51.780
I was gonna say that Mike and I bought a house together.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:54.520
I know, you know, it's just been a while.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:58.920
That might be kind of a shock to you.

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:01.440
I just wanted to let you know.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:05.100
But don't worry, Joe, because you can read all about the house on the internet.

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:07.180
It's true. Yeah, I figured there'd be a website.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:11.360
And, you know, it does hurt that my ex-roommate has found another roommate.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:12.820
But what are you gonna do?

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:14.720
Trust me, my wife was surprised, too.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:16.980
Yep. You bought a zoo.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:21.040
But that's just what it's like living in Neo-Detroit these days.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:22.860
Is it Neo?

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:24.940
It's not Neo. It's not Neo-Detroit.

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.360
It's Detroit in the distant future.

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:31.020
And, like, I'm sure we made a joke about this in the Robocop episode.

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:39.040
But it's like, man, it's really hard to imagine, you know, Detroit being under such tough circumstances, right?

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:42.120
I think that was the joke even then.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:51.680
Okay, good. Because when I was rewatching the films for this episode, I was kind of like, damn, like, it kind of happened.

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:53.100
Yeah, yeah.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:56.720
I mean, like I said, it happened already.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:58.560
Great. Yeah, it was on its way.

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:09.840
Yeah. And I don't know if you guys remember the original Robocop game, but the biggest issue that we had with it was that you could not jump as Robocop.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:16.380
And I don't know if they were trying to say that the armor is just so heavy or if they didn't think that the technology existed on the NES yet.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:18.720
They maybe hadn't played Super Mario Brothers.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:26.300
But Robocop 2, thankfully, introduces jumping to the series.

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:30.000
But it is kind of a monkey's paw wish, right?

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:33.340
Yes. Like, oh, I really wish they had jumping in Robocop.

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:34.880
And what did they do about it, Sean?

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:47.320
Well, I think that they had, you know, you're probably right in thinking that the reason that you can't jump is because the armor was so heavy or he's just such a hulking mess.

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:58.200
Uh, so instead of doing it again like that, they instead have this just sort of slicky, slidey, every surface.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:11.720
Whereas I think that they're trying to make it just feel like you are a guy with weight, but it just makes it feel like the entire game is covered in sludge because you slide all over the place here now.

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:25.020
Yeah, when you land and you kind of slide, like, I think they do a good job, like, of in a illustration way of, like, showing you, oh, yeah, look, you have all this weight because you're in this armor.

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:28.660
I think it's good in that sense, but it's not good in a game design sense.

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:33.480
Like, it doesn't, it's not like, doesn't make it more fun, but I am like, okay, yeah, I get why.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:40.780
I don't know if I necessarily get why I slide so much, but, like, I get why that's hard to stop myself when I have this momentum from jumping.

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:46.720
And we've praised sliding in some games that involve, you know, jumping as the core mechanic.

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:52.000
Notably, Super Mario Brothers has a little bit of, you know, a swing in your momentum as you're landing.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:04:04.540
This one has a little bit of a different approach, though, where the sliding and startup of your movement is just as slippery and just as slow to begin as it is to stop it.

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:09.060
So you have to kind of, like, commit to, like, okay, I'm going to start moving now.

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:14.180
And, like, you're moving at, like, a snail's pace, and then you start ramping up in speed.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:20.160
But there's never, like, there's not any long platforms or anything that are like, yeah, sure, here's your runway.

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:21.580
Get ready for takeoff.

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:26.140
It's all, like, pretty short, like, precision platforming.

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:30.940
And so I question why he needs to, like, he's, like, Scooby-Doo.

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:34.160
He's, like, running in place for a little bit before he, like, gets moving.

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:38.360
I think they just overdid it in every direction.

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:46.660
Yeah, because I do think that a little bit of sliding after, like, stopping is, it does help it feel like you have the right weight, like in Mario.

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:53.160
And even Mario does have, like, a little bit of, like, it takes some time to build momentum.

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:56.640
But, like, it's not noticeable because it shouldn't be noticeable.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:58.400
It should just be, like, intuitive feeling.

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:01.320
This is very noticeable and very frustrating.

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:05.160
It's just like they cranked the sliders up to 11 here.

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:08.800
And that's, it makes it feel very bad.

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:10.520
Bad is the good word here.

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:12.900
And so you're obviously playing as RoboCop.

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:25.160
You're chasing down the drug nuke, which, let's be honest, if they ever decided to call, you know, a drug formally referred to it as nuke, kids all over America would be trying to find that shit right away.

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:27.460
Like, the drug is nuke?

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:28.440
That's insane.

00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:32.560
That's awesome. But you have to destroy all that stuff.

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:44.600
And you also have to, you know, kind of, just like any good cop, meet your quotas across the stage of destroying a certain amount of nuke and arresting a certain amount of criminals.

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:51.040
You miss either quota and you're kind of thrown into the shooting gallery training stage.

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:54.200
Sometimes. To make sure that, yeah, exactly, to make sure you're a good cop.

00:05:54.200 --> 00:06:04.360
RoboCop. But this is, you know, coming off of the RoboCop 2 movie, which, you know, are either of you familiar with it?

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:05.400
Have you seen it before?

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:08.400
Did you watch a little clip for this episode?

00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:10.980
Yeah, I mean, I liked RoboCop 1.

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:16.080
And then I was debating watching RoboCop 2 for this episode.

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:19.120
And then I watched, like, some highlights.

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:21.780
And then I was like, I don't need to see RoboCop 2.

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:27.100
It's pretty goofy.

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:29.780
Like, in a fun way, like now.

00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:35.340
But I feel like if I was watching it when it came out, I would find it just, like, the bad kind of goofy.

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:41.860
It was certainly a choice to have RoboCop 2, which is the name of the new RoboCop in the movie.

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:47.060
It was a choice to have RoboCop 2 be entirely stop-motion animation.

00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:48.960
Yeah, that was pretty bad. Wait, is it a different character?

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:53.160
Oh, yeah. RoboCop 2 was not just the name of the game and the sequel.

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:58.340
But RoboCop 2 is the second version of RoboCop.

00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:03.260
Right. I kind of gathered that somehow while playing this game.

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:06.700
I did not watch the movie. But somehow I did gather, oh, RoboCop 2 is the name of a RoboCop.

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:12.340
I thought it was going to be, like, the enemy of the movie was RoboCop 2.

00:07:12.340 --> 00:07:14.740
And RoboCop needed to, like, defeat him or something.

00:07:14.980 --> 00:07:16.740
That is the plot. That is what happens, yes.

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:19.180
Okay, okay. Yeah, see, RoboCop 2 is in...

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:21.540
So, there's still the same character as RoboCop as our hero.

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:22.620
Yes, exactly.

00:07:23.020 --> 00:07:24.980
RoboCop is the humanoid.

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:31.000
And then RoboCop 2 is literally just, like, you know, a giant hulking machine thing that has, like, a screen for a face.

00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:32.380
And a brain in it.

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:44.780
Yeah. Brain in a jar. But it was, like, but the fatal flaw, of course, was that it was supposed to be, the brains were supposed to be dead police officers so that they could continue to serve their city.

00:07:45.220 --> 00:07:52.840
And instead, you know, the bright woman in charge of it all was, like, you know what, I think we should have criminals' brains in here instead.

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:54.980
Because they have a lust for power.

00:07:55.620 --> 00:07:56.960
And they'll, like, what?

00:07:56.960 --> 00:08:11.280
Well, I mean, you know, if you, you know, I did see the scene where they kept trying with the normal people and all of their, you know, removed from their body brains would just immediately kill themselves.

00:08:11.300 --> 00:08:15.380
So, it's like, you gotta find someone that has lust for power and immortality.

00:08:15.980 --> 00:08:17.320
So, obviously, Death Row inmates.

00:08:17.320 --> 00:08:18.640
Only criminals. Yeah, exactly.

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:23.680
I gotta tell you, RoboCop 2, total different direction than I ever expected just from this conversation.

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:33.600
Well, here's what happened, Joe, is that the original screenwriters for RoboCop 1 were going to write RoboCop 2, but then the writers' guild strike happened.

00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:38.780
And they were fired because they refused to work during the guild strike.

00:08:39.160 --> 00:08:41.640
So, somehow, Frank Miller comes on board.

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:45.220
You know, the guy from Daredevil and Batman and all that stuff.

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:47.060
He gets on board as the screenwriter.

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:48.760
He writes a first draft.

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:49.860
It gets rejected.

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:56.920
The second draft becomes RoboCop 2, but that first draft that was rejected, don't worry, that gets used for RoboCop 3.

00:08:56.920 --> 00:08:59.160
I've never heard of that happening before.

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:02.040
Like, that's how different the two drafts were?

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:06.460
And this tracks is Frank Miller.

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:10.040
I mean, the writing mind of 300 right here.

00:09:10.420 --> 00:09:11.660
Yes, there you go.

00:09:12.940 --> 00:09:25.280
And, you know, I don't know if any of these plot points, like, would, you know, in the movie would matter because, Joe, since you didn't watch the movie, the game wasn't like you weren't following what was going down, right?

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:30.820
Like, you have these nice screens at the end of each level that tell you you have destroyed this percentage of the nuke.

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:32.980
You have arrested this percentage of the targets.

00:09:34.540 --> 00:09:47.740
It's so, you know, that's enough of, like, it's a good objective for both, like, a way to translate the movie and to gamify it into, like, an almost arcade-like experience rather than trying to go for, like, some cinematic beats.

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:52.580
Like we saw with Untouchables and other games, like, oh, we should just cover the moments from the movie.

00:09:52.580 --> 00:09:54.000
Like, this is the good way of doing it.

00:09:54.220 --> 00:10:06.460
The hard part is, is that they don't, like, you need 60% of the nukes and 60% of the criminals arrested to, like, move on in the stage.

00:10:06.520 --> 00:10:12.820
But that's not, that number isn't, like, that percentage number is not communicated anywhere on the screen until you get to the end.

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:15.080
And you don't know you're approaching the end of the screen.

00:10:15.160 --> 00:10:20.960
There's not even, like, some kind of timeline of, like, where you are in the level and you can't go backwards, like, once you pass.

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:28.180
The whole can't go backwards is actually unforgivable here if you're going to have us have a quota.

00:10:29.500 --> 00:10:39.300
And just the way that the maps are laid out, there's usually, like, it's not, like, diverging paths in, like, a fun way.

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:46.500
It's just, like, you can either take a high platform route or a low near the bottom of the screen route.

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:53.140
And, but, like, if you go one way or the other, you are effectively locked out of the other way.

00:10:53.900 --> 00:10:56.520
And there's going to be nuke on both of those levels.

00:10:58.020 --> 00:11:00.940
And you can't go back to try and go get the rest.

00:11:01.100 --> 00:11:02.460
It's pretty insane.

00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:03.760
Yeah. It's bizarre.

00:11:04.040 --> 00:11:07.960
Because, honestly, I don't remember a game in a long time where you can't backtrack.

00:11:08.140 --> 00:11:09.420
Since, like, Super Mario Bros.

00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:18.020
1. Like, so, like, why in this game, the one where, like, it's, you know, you're supposed to be collecting these things, some that are just out of reach.

00:11:18.620 --> 00:11:20.860
Or, like, yeah, just somewhere there's, oh, there's two.

00:11:20.980 --> 00:11:22.860
I have to pick one and now I can't go back for the other.

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:27.880
It's just impossible. Well, Joe, you'll have to forgive them because they just figured out, they just discovered jumping.

00:11:28.980 --> 00:11:35.140
That's true. They're really on the slow path to, like, figuring out game mechanics.

00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:43.400
But the other thing about the not going backwards that drives me crazy is coupling it with the slidiness of it all.

00:11:43.960 --> 00:11:46.880
Where, like, or the, like, kind of momentum you need to build up.

00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:52.800
Where sometimes you're on a platform where you can get something that's just ahead of you.

00:11:52.940 --> 00:11:55.920
You can go back to that platform to try and, like, jump up higher.

00:11:56.100 --> 00:12:01.880
But now you can't, not enough of the platform is uncovered for you to get a running start.

00:12:01.880 --> 00:12:03.620
Because you've gone too far forward.

00:12:03.620 --> 00:12:05.600
It's almost like a soft lock, yeah.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:06.900
Yeah, yeah.

00:12:07.160 --> 00:12:09.040
And it's just, like, I don't know.

00:12:09.100 --> 00:12:14.820
It's a bizarre, like, combination of bad things that makes it even worse thing.

00:12:15.100 --> 00:12:24.180
And probably the worst part of it all, too, is, like, you could think that you're collecting enough nuke and arresting enough people or collecting enough people, depending on the level.

00:12:25.320 --> 00:12:27.600
And then just finding out, like...

00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:30.100
Hostages. Yeah, yeah, collecting the hostages.

00:12:31.340 --> 00:12:32.980
You could think that you're doing a good job.

00:12:32.980 --> 00:12:35.640
And then you just find out, like, you know, no, you didn't.

00:12:35.780 --> 00:12:37.080
Like, at the end of the stage.

00:12:37.160 --> 00:12:39.040
It's just, like, such a weird time to find out.

00:12:39.220 --> 00:12:40.380
Like, nope, you failed.

00:12:40.520 --> 00:12:52.880
Go do it again. It does kind of give you a warning at the beginning of some of the stages that they're, like, hey, there's, like, a secret nuke cache somewhere in this level.

00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:57.860
Which tells you there's going to be, like, a hidden room that is not telegraphed at all.

00:12:58.780 --> 00:13:02.200
Where a good percentage of the total nuke on the stage.

00:13:02.320 --> 00:13:03.520
Like, it's not bonus nuke.

00:13:03.700 --> 00:13:06.280
It's counted in the percentage calculation.

00:13:07.820 --> 00:13:09.920
But you could very easily miss it.

00:13:10.020 --> 00:13:12.620
Like, I see... I was watching a speed run.

00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:14.040
Or not speed, just a long play.

00:13:14.260 --> 00:13:18.840
And saw on the first level there is, like, a secret area that I completely missed.

00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:23.640
And then you end up having to collect a much higher percentage of what's actually on the screen.

00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:27.920
So, Sean, did you beat the first level without going to that secret area?

00:13:28.460 --> 00:13:30.060
Yes. Oh, wow.

00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:32.340
Okay, so I played the first level.

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:36.560
I didn't. I had to do the gun game.

00:13:37.020 --> 00:13:38.060
Yeah, but then it still...

00:13:38.060 --> 00:13:39.300
Doesn't it still send you back?

00:13:39.460 --> 00:13:41.520
Or if you do well enough on the gun game, do you then...

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:43.580
If you do well enough on the gun game, you go forward.

00:13:43.740 --> 00:13:45.480
I've never done well enough on the gun game.

00:13:45.940 --> 00:13:48.420
I played that, like, four times.

00:13:48.620 --> 00:13:50.800
And then played the first level, like, four times.

00:13:51.060 --> 00:13:53.520
Kept failing until I finally watched someone play it.

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:55.620
And found the secret cache of it.

00:13:55.740 --> 00:13:57.080
And that was the only way I was able to beat it.

00:13:57.360 --> 00:14:00.580
But then sometimes when I didn't beat it, it didn't take me to that bonus level anyways.

00:14:00.900 --> 00:14:04.720
So, I don't really know what determines that factor.

00:14:04.880 --> 00:14:07.560
Yeah, I'm not sure. Joe, can I ask you a question about the gun game?

00:14:07.900 --> 00:14:11.520
Yeah. This is what happens if you don't get the 60% for each.

00:14:11.520 --> 00:14:14.420
You get sent to kind of like this Hogan's Alley style.

00:14:15.520 --> 00:14:18.400
What would be great with a zapper, but now you have to do it with a D-pad.

00:14:19.860 --> 00:14:20.960
You see your crosshair.

00:14:21.160 --> 00:14:22.560
You have all these cardboard targets.

00:14:22.560 --> 00:14:26.220
It's just as slippery as moving RoboCop.

00:14:26.480 --> 00:14:31.800
Yes, exactly. But I think that the slipperiness of this is the one time in this game that it works.

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:36.160
I think it works better than other games that we've had that are like shimby zapper games.

00:14:36.160 --> 00:14:39.400
I don't know. I said it with some stank on it.

00:14:40.260 --> 00:14:44.480
It's just funny that it's still there even when he's not even on the screen.

00:14:45.060 --> 00:14:46.840
And here's why I agree with it too.

00:14:46.940 --> 00:14:55.180
And what I was going to ask you, Joe, is like the first time you did the shooting gallery, did you treat your gun like it was a pistol instead of a machine gun?

00:14:55.460 --> 00:14:56.720
I think I did the opposite.

00:14:57.080 --> 00:14:59.580
The first time I did it, I treated it like it was a machine gun.

00:14:59.580 --> 00:15:07.480
And then I realized I can be a little more conservative with my ammo if I just like fire as I'm going across the nuke or the enemies.

00:15:08.220 --> 00:15:14.000
Right, because I was treating it more like you had to like line up the crosshairs and then shoot these cardboard targets.

00:15:14.220 --> 00:15:17.740
And then like if, you know, if you're doing that, by the time you get some of these guys, they're gone.

00:15:17.760 --> 00:15:21.480
And then you're looking for the next person because it's not all on one confined screen.

00:15:21.480 --> 00:15:22.680
The screen scrolls across.

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:25.040
So there's always targets coming and going.

00:15:25.780 --> 00:15:31.080
But because it's a machine gun, you can kind of like as you're approaching targets, start firing.

00:15:31.340 --> 00:15:32.420
And I think that's kind of intended.

00:15:32.420 --> 00:15:42.560
So this way it's like, you know, you're not going to have like a perfect shooting rate, but you're going to hit a lot more of these enemies when you do it that way.

00:15:42.680 --> 00:15:45.460
And so once I did that, then it was like, OK, cool.

00:15:45.560 --> 00:15:50.220
I'm out of shooting gallery jail and I can go back to playing the main game.

00:15:50.260 --> 00:16:02.060
And then I was like, well, maybe I want to be back at the shooting gallery, actually, because the main game continues to just do the same thing over and over again with this, you know, lack of communication and stuff.

00:16:02.060 --> 00:16:12.920
But with trickier and trickier platforming as time goes on, like it really becomes a chore to get through a lot of these stages, starting as early as that third stage with that like hover.

00:16:13.840 --> 00:16:15.600
Oh, the flapping bird knockoff.

00:16:15.820 --> 00:16:16.780
Yeah, yeah, exactly.

00:16:17.060 --> 00:16:21.480
And all these things that can kill you but don't look like they should kill RoboCop.

00:16:21.660 --> 00:16:24.400
Like RoboCop is very vulnerable in this game.

00:16:24.680 --> 00:16:30.320
Yeah. And also there's a lot of things that are platform that look like platforms that aren't platforms that you'll just fall through.

00:16:30.320 --> 00:16:36.760
